  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 1128 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe amplifying ridge over the Southwest will maintain dry, calm\nconditons through the end of the work week. Temperatures will\nremain near or above normal with light winds and periods of high \nclouds.\n\nThings become more active as we reach Friday and head into the\nweekend as a pair of Pacific systems move towards the region. The\nfirst of the two looks to travel south of the Land of Enchantment\nand across northern Mexico. This will allow for some moisture \nadvection northward into the CWA, but limited precipitation is \nexpected. The second, stronger disturbance is forecast to \npropagate across the northern half of the state Saturday into \nSunday night, bringing the best opportunity for precipitation out \nof the two systems. This is particularly true for the western and \nnorthern mountains. Valley rain and high elevation snow (above \n8500-9000 ft) will also be possible. Breezy to windy conditions \nare also expected, as long-term guidance shows 40-50 knots 700 mb\nwinds overspreading the region. The highest winds at the surface \nwill set up along and east of the Central Mountain Chain, where \nnorth-south oriented roads could see crosswind gusts between 35-50\nmph. \n\nA brief period of quieter weather is anticipated Sunday into\nMonday as brief ridging moves across the region. However, another\nsystem looks to approach the western CONUS as early as next\nTuesday. This could bring more precipitation chances to the state\nas early as Tuesday, although uncertainty does remain regarding \nthe eventual track and timing of this disturbance.",
